body:
## What problem are you trying to solve?

Ranges are one of these Web APIs that have been infamously difficult to work with. Their API is centered around nodes and offsets within individual nodes:
- `startContainer`: The `Node` containing the start boundary point
- `startOffset`: The offset within `startContainer`. 
- `endContainer`: The `Node` containing the end boundary point
- `endOffset`: The offset within `endContainer`

Spec: https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-abstractrange

It gets worse: **what `[start|end]Offset` mean depends on the type of `[start|end]Container`!** If `[start|end]Container` is an element node, the offset represents **a child node index** otherwise it's a character offset! 🫠

This makes complex things possible, but simple things are _very_ hard.
Many use cases are node-agnostic, and operate on overall character offsets. This currently requires _extensive_ amounts of logic.

### What solutions exist today?

E.g. to _get_ the current offsets, you need a TreeWalker (!):

```js
function getRangeOffsets(range, root = range.commonAncestorContainer) {
  if (root.nodeType === Node.TEXT_NODE) {
    return { start: range.startOffset, end: range.endOffset };
  }

  const { startContainer, startOffset } = range;
  const isText = startContainer.nodeType === Node.TEXT_NODE;
  const boundary = isText ? startContainer : startContainer.childNodes[startOffset] ?? null;

  const walker = document.createTreeWalker(root, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT);
  let start = isText ? startOffset : 0;
  let node;
  while ((node = walker.nextNode())) {
    if (boundary && (node === boundary ||
        (node.compareDocumentPosition(boundary) & Node.DOCUMENT_POSITION_PRECEDING))) break;
    start += node.data.length;
  }

  return { start, end: start + range.toString().length };
}
```

There is a hack to get the UA's internal measurements, but it involves **creating another range**:

```js
function getRangeOffsets(range, root = range.commonAncestorContainer) {
  const pre = document.createRange();
  pre.selectNodeContents(root);
  pre.setEnd(range.startContainer, range.startOffset);
  const start = pre.toString().length;
  return { start, end: start + range.toString().length };
}
```

It gets even worse if you want to _set_ the offsets, with **a tree walk being your _only_ choice**:

```js
function setRangeOffsets(range, root, { start, end }) {
  const locate = (offset) => {
    if (root.nodeType === Node.TEXT_NODE) {
      return [root, Math.min(Math.max(offset, 0), root.data.length)];
    }
    const walker = document.createTreeWalker(root, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT);
    let remaining = offset, node, last = null;
    while ((node = walker.nextNode())) {
      if (remaining <= node.data.length) return [node, remaining];
      remaining -= node.data.length;
      last = node;
    }
    // Offset past the end of all text: clamp to end.
    return last ? [last, last.data.length] : [root, 0];
  };
  range.setStart(...locate(start));
  range.setEnd(...locate(end));
}
```

## How would you solve it?

A new pair of IDL attributes to read and write the **overall** character offsets. Potential names:
- `characterStartOffset` / `characterEndOffset`
- `textStart` / `textEnd`
- `start` / `end`
- ...

These would follow the shape of existing boundary attributes:
- writable on mutable Range objects, read-only on immutable Range objects
- live on live ranges, not live on static ranges

This could also address the `OpaqueRange` dilemma discussed today: instead of exposing `startOffset` and `endOffset`, which have a different meaning depending on whether the boundaries are element nodes, or contorting them to mean something _different_ for `OpaqueRange`, only expose these high-level attributes which have the same meaning regardless — and if we want to expose the rest later, we still can, and these are independently useful.

A big open question is **what is `startOffset` relative to?**

- **The Range's `commonAncestorContainer`?** Convenient but unstable. Moving an endpoint can shift the reference frame, so `textStart` could change without semantically moving. Almost certainly wrong.
- **The Range's root?** That's a document or shadow root, too wide to be useful in most use cases. Also expensive on large documents/subtrees.
- **New `textContainer` or `scope` IDL attribute** to set the intended container of the range (e.g. the editor in a `contentEditable` area). Offsets outside of this container are clamped to `[0, container.textContent.length]`. Defaults to `<body>` for document ranges and the shadow root host on shadow root ranges. Larger scope, but easier to match use cases, and more semantically expressive. Can also be used for performance optimizations.
- **Instead of a pair of IDL attributes, two methods:** `range.getTextOffsets()` and `range.setTextOffsets()` (for non immutable ranges). These can accept the root as an option, though this makes it harder to optimize. 

## Non-goals

- Distinguishing between visible and non-visible text. The invariant to preserve is that `range.textEnd - range.textStart === range.toString().length`.

## Example use cases

### Code highlighting

This comes up a lot in syntax highlighting, since parsers require actual source code, regardless of what HTML elements may have been used to format it.
This applies to both regular ranges (for highlighting in a `<code>` element) or `OpaqueRange` (for highlighting code in a `<textarea>`).

### `selectionStart` / `selectionEnd` for `contentEditable`

Every `contentEditable` editor has had to reinvent this.

### Selection persistence across re-renders

Every framework-driven editor (React, Vue, Lit, Svelte) loses the user's caret/selection when the editable subtree re-renders, because the saved (node, offset) pairs reference nodes that no longer exist. The standard workaround is "save as offsets before patch, restore after" — currently requires the TreeWalker dance on both sides of every render. Same pattern for innerHTML rewrites, Markdown round-trips, and undo/redo stacks where the snapshot includes selection state.

### External text services

Spellcheckers, grammar checkers, LanguageTool, Hemingway-style linters, and increasingly LLM editing endpoints (`{ start: 142, end: 158, replacement: "…" }`) all speak in offsets — they receive a flat string and return spans. Mapping those spans back to Ranges for highlighting via CSS Custom Highlight API is currently the friction point everyone in this space hits.

### Persistent annotations

The [W3C Web Annotation Data Model's TextPositionSelector](https://www.w3.org/TR/annotation-model/#text-position-selector) is defined as { start, end } integer offsets, precisely because (node, offset) doesn't survive serialization, page reloads, or minor DOM changes. Hypothes.is, Readwise, and similar tools implement offset↔Range conversion on every page load. (Bonus: anchoring algorithms that recover annotations after edits also operate in offset space.)

### Collaborative editing

OT and most text CRDTs (Yjs's Y.Text, Automerge text, Loro) model positions as integer offsets in the shared document. Bidirectional Range↔offset conversion runs on every cursor movement, every remote operation application, and every local change broadcast — usually the hottest path in a collaborative editor.

### `Intl.Segmenter` composition

`Intl.Segmenter` returns grapheme/word/sentence boundaries as integer offsets into a string. With these attributes, "select the next sentence" or "delete the previous word" in a contenteditable becomes segmenter.segment(root.textContent) + property write. Without them, every editor reimplements word/sentence boundary heuristics — usually incorrectly for non-Latin scripts. (The MS RangeInnerText explainer leans heavily on this composability argument; worth borrowing.)

### Find-on-page / in-app search

Regex or substring search over `root.textContent` yields offset ranges. Highlighting hits via Custom Highlight API needs the inverse mapping. 

### Editors and text controls

Editors frequently maintain selections as linear offsets, especially when their document model is text-first or token-first rather than DOM-first.

This comes up for:

- rich text editors
- code editors
- Markdown editors with rendered previews
- diff viewers
- generated or virtualized content

The DOM is often a projection of the editor model, not the model itself, so node-relative boundary points are the wrong abstraction level.
